% ============================================================================
% onset-rime — a word's onset and rime split, a FOURTH literacy sub-skill in
% the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Deliberately DIFFERENT in shape from all three prior literacy libraries:
% `word-families.adj` derives RHYMING (RF.K.2.a) via a `rule`,
% `syllable-count.adj` recalls a SYLLABLE COUNT (RF.K.2.b), and
% `initial-sound.adj` recalls a word's BEGINNING sound (RF.K.2.d). This
% library recalls how a single-syllable word splits into its ONSET (the
% sound(s) before the vowel) and RIME (the vowel and everything after),
% grounding RF.K.2.c ("Blend and segment onsets and rimes of single-syllable
% spoken words") as a pure lookup: `onset_rime(word, onset, rime)`. Recall is
% a binding query:
%
%     ? onset_rime(sleep, $Onset, $Rime)   % sl, eep
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a THREE-column table like
% `metrology/si-base-units.adj`): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `onset_rime(word, onset, rime)` carrying the Reading Rockets citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% both parts WITH their source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word not one of the two the cited page splits. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind an onset and rime and recall the word they
% blend into (the classic "blending" exercise, the inverse of "segmenting"):
%
%     ? onset_rime($Word, bl, ast)   % blast
%
% The four words and their splits, as a little truth table:
%
%     word     onset   rime   the cited split
%     ------   -----   ----   -------------------------------------
%     sleep    sl      eep    "sleep could be broken into /sl/ and /eep/"
%     blast    bl      ast    "Onset (bl) – Rime (ast)"
%     map      m       ap     "in the word 'map,' /m/ is the onset and /ap/
%                               is the rime" (added this cycle)
%     tape     t       ape    "The word is 'tape'. The first part is /t/ ...
%                               /Ape/" (added this cycle)
%
% EXTENDED this cycle from 2 to 4 rows: discovered while scoping the next
% literacy slice, a fresh WebFetch of Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module -- the SAME already-vetted page
% `syllable-count.adj`/`phoneme-substitution.adj`/`phoneme-deletion.adj`/
% `syllable-substitution.adj`/`syllable-deletion.adj` already cite -- surfaced
% its "Blending Onset and Rime" and "Onset-rime Completion" sections, each
% naming a word/onset/rime triple in the SAME `onset_rime(word, onset, rime)`
% shape this table already carries. Both new rows are a pure addition; the
% original `sleep`/`blast` rows are untouched. WebFetch-verified THREE
% separate times for consistency before writing, all byte-identical.
%
% Provenance: `sleep` and `blast` are quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets'
% "Tuning In to the Sounds in Words" article, WebFetch-verified TWICE for
% consistency before writing (as originally shipped). `map` and `tape` are
% quoted verbatim from the "In Practice" module's onset/rime sections above.
% The exact spans:
%
%   sleep -> sl / eep:
%     "One way to break down the syllable is into onset (everything before
%      the vowel) and rime (the vowel and everything after it). For example,
%      sleep could be broken into /sl/ and /eep/."
%     — https://www.readingrockets.org/topics/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/articles/tuning-sounds-words
%   blast -> bl / ast:
%     "Here are two ways to break up the word blast: Onset (bl) – Rime (ast)"
%     — https://www.readingrockets.org/topics/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/articles/tuning-sounds-words
%   map -> m / ap (added this cycle):
%     "So in the word 'map,' /m/ is the onset and /ap/ is the rime."
%     — https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice
%   tape -> t / ape (added this cycle):
%     "The word is 'tape'. The first part is /t/ ... What's the rest of the
%      word? /Ape/."
%     — https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single strongest span: the original `sleep` sentence
% from the "Tuning In to the Sounds in Words" article. Every OTHER row's
% per-fact source and verbatim span is listed above, so each split remains
% independently checkable. `trust consensus` — Reading Rockets is a
% widely-used literacy parent/educator resource, the same tier
% `word-families.adj`'s and `syllable-count.adj`'s Reading Rockets sources
% earned. A future slice can add a fifth split once a comparably clean
% citation is found. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table onset_rime {
    columns word, onset, rime

    row (sleep, sl, eep)
    row (blast, bl, ast)
    row (map, m, ap)
    row (tape, t, ape)

    source "For example, sleep could be broken into /sl/ and /eep/."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/topics/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/articles/tuning-sounds-words"
    trust consensus
}
